<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="HW12" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Homework 12</title>

  <p><alert> Due Thursday, May 14</alert></p>

<exercises xml:id="hw12-ex">
<exercise>
<p>
Consider the polynomials <m>f(x,y,z)=x^3-x^2y-x^2z+x</m> and <m>f_1=x^2y-z</m> and <m>f_2=xy-1</m>. 
  </p>
<ol>
  <li>
<p> Using lex order, divide <m>f</m> by <m>(f_1,f_2) </m> and record the quotient and remainder below: 
<me> f(x,y,z)=\ldots\ldots\ldots\cdot(x^2y-z)+\ldots\ldots\ldots \cdot(xy-1)+\ldots\ldots\ldots</me>
</p>
</li>
<li>
  <p>
    Now reverse the order. That is, divide <m> f</m> by <m>(f_2, f_1) </m> and record the quotient and remainder below: 
    <me> f(x,y,z)=\ldots\ldots\ldots\cdot(xy-1)+\ldots\ldots\ldots \cdot(x^2y-z)+\ldots\ldots\ldots</me>
  </p>
</li>
</ol>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
Consider the ideal <m> I=(x-z^2, y-z^3)
  </m> of <m>K[x,y,z]</m>.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        Is <m>G=\{x-z^2,y-z^3\}</m> a Groebner basis for <m>I</m> under the lex order with <m> x>y>z</m>?  
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Is <m>G=\{x-z^2,y-z^3\}</m> a Groebner basis for <m>I</m> under the lex order with <m> z>y>x</m>?  
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Is <m>G=\{x-z^2,y-z^3\}</m> a Groebner basis for <m>I</m> under the grlex (graded lex) order with <m> x>y>z</m>?  
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  </exercise>


</exercises>
</section>